<section class="audience-report" data-details aria-labelledby="details-title">
 <div class="panel-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Understand your visitors</p><h2 id="details-title">Where they come from. What they do.</h2></div></div>
 <p class="detail-note" data-details-coverage>Loading detailed analytics…</p>
 <p class="detail-note" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-details-status></p>
 <nav class="detail-nav" aria-label="Analytics details"><a href="#traffic-sources">Sources</a><a href="#time-spent">Time spent</a><a href="#popular-pages">Pages &amp; tabs</a><a href="#exit-pages">Exits</a><a href="#new-returning">New &amp; returning</a></nav>
 <div class="detail-grid">
  <section class="detail-panel" id="traffic-sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title"><p class="eyebrow">1 · Where visits come from</p><h2 id="sources-title">Traffic sources</h2><p class="detail-note">Google, Facebook, Instagram, shared links and other referrals. A source shows how someone arrived; it does not reveal their motivation. Some apps hide the source, so social shares can appear as Direct / unknown.</p><h3>Recorded arrivals</h3><div data-detail-sources></div><details class="historical-detail" open><summary>Earlier source estimates</summary><p class="detail-note">Cloudflare page opens by referring site, including internal links. These are separate from recorded visits.</p><div data-history-sources></div></details></section>
  <section class="detail-panel" id="time-spent" aria-labelledby="time-title"><p class="eyebrow">2 · How long they stay</p><h2 id="time-title">Active time on site</h2><strong class="detail-number" data-active-time>—</strong><p class="detail-note">Average active time per recorded visit</p><div class="detail-pair"><p><strong data-active-total>—</strong><span>Total active time</span></p><p><strong data-detail-visits>—</strong><span>Recorded visits</span></p></div><p class="detail-note">Counts visible, focused browsing. Pauses after 60 seconds without interaction unless a video is playing. Background tabs do not add time. A visit ends after 30 minutes of inactivity; an open visit’s time is still growing.</p></section>
  <section class="detail-panel detail-wide" id="popular-pages" aria-labelledby="pages-title"><p class="eyebrow">3 · Which pages &amp; tabs they visit</p><h2 id="pages-title">Popular pages</h2><p class="detail-note">Page opens include repeat loads. Average active time is calculated for each measured page stay; scroll depth is the average furthest point reached.</p><div data-detail-pages></div><details class="historical-detail" open><summary>Earlier page estimates</summary><div data-history-pages></div></details></section>
  <section class="detail-panel" id="exit-pages" aria-labelledby="exits-title"><p class="eyebrow">4 · Where visits end</p><h2 id="exits-title">Exit pages &amp; form problems</h2><div class="detail-pair"><p><strong data-completed-visits>—</strong><span>Finished visits</span></p><p><strong data-short-visits>—</strong><span>One-page visits under 10s active</span></p></div><div data-detail-exits></div><p class="detail-note">An exit is the last page in a visit inactive for at least 30 minutes. Unfinished forms, observed form errors and outward clicks are clues, not proven reasons for leaving. An outward click can be a successful contact or social visit. No form answers are collected.</p></section>
  <section class="detail-panel" id="new-returning" aria-labelledby="audience-title"><p class="eyebrow">5 · First visits &amp; return visits</p><h2 id="audience-title">New &amp; returning</h2><div class="audience-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i data-new-bar></i><i data-returning-bar></i><i data-unknown-bar></i></div><div class="detail-pair"><p><strong data-new-visits>—</strong><span>New visits</span></p><p><strong data-returning-visits>—</strong><span>Returning visits</span></p></div><p class="detail-note" data-audience-note>Waiting for recorded visits.</p><p class="detail-note">“New” means a browser’s first known visit; another visit from that browser is “returning”. Reloading within the same visit adds a page open. Clearing storage, changing devices or the identifier expiring can make a browser look new again. Privacy signals or blocked storage produce unidentified visits.</p></section>
  <section class="detail-panel detail-wide" aria-labelledby="audience-dates-title"><h2 id="audience-dates-title" data-audience-title>Daily visit breakdown</h2><p class="detail-note">Uses the selected dates and grouping above. Visits and their engagement are assigned to the date the visit began.</p><div data-detail-daily></div></section>
 </div>
</section>
<style is:global>
